The three ladders: revelation, translation, and ethical resistance\
The proposed Blake\uc0\u8594 Climacus replacement in the fold plan should not be applied. The board contains William Blake\'92s Jacob\'92s Dream, John Climacus\'92s Ladder of Divine Ascent, and Robert Fludd\'92s cognitive ladder in separate views.\
\
The British Museum identifies Blake\'92s image as an illustration of Genesis 28:12, with figures both ascending and descending. The museum record confirms the current Bethel/Jacob basis.\
\
Climacus is not an unrelated monastic substitute for Jacob. The Greek Orthodox Archdiocese describes his thirty-step treatise as the ladder contemplated by Jacob in Genesis 28:12. Its account makes the relationship explicit.\
\
Fludd\'92s 1619 ladder moves from sense through imagination, reason, intellect, intelligence, and Word toward the divine. An academic treatment identifies it as another adaptation of Jacob\'92s dream and lays out those stages. The UCLouvain paper supports that identification.\
\
The three images are not redundant:\
\
Ladder	Operation supplied to the project\
Blake	Revelatory traffic: can earth and heaven communicate, and can movement occur in both directions?\
Fludd	Epistemic translation: how does sensory and embodied experience become imagination, reason, intelligence, and Word?\
Climacus	Ethical resistance: can ascent survive distraction, ego, temptation, status, and falling?\
NChSh hopscotch	Embodied play: can the cosmic path be lived with feet, risk, recurrence, and childhood movement?\
